<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ogun</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ògún, Ogum, Ogou</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ogun is the deity of iron, metalworking and war in the traditional religions of several West African peoples, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a>, Edo and Itsekiri. In Yoruba religion he is a primordial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orisha">orisha</a> who, in some traditions, cleared a path for the other orishas to come to Earth with a metal machete, helped by a dog. Scholars trace his worship to the Yoruba, but the historian Sandra T. Barnes holds that the time and place of his emergence are uncertain and the evidence circumstantial. His worship spread to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, where in Brazilian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/candomble">Candomblé</a> he is known as Ogum and identified with Saint George.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ogun.jpg" alt="Ogun" width="330" height="414" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: James Ajala · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ogun_Helmet_Mask.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
